## CURSOR IDE BEST PRACTICES

### Rule File Structure:
- ✅ Single source of truth (modular rule files)
- ✅ Proper YAML frontmatter
- ✅ Clear organization (focused modules, each under 500 lines)
- ✅ No conflicts or duplication
- ✅ Actionable rules with examples
- ✅ Cross-references between modules

### Rule File Naming:
- Use descriptive names: `CORE-AUTOMATION.mdc`, `GIT-WORKFLOW.mdc`, etc.
- Use consistent versioning: `version: "4.0"`
- Use `alwaysApply: true` for all rule files

---

## GIT WORKFLOW BEST PRACTICES

### PR-Centric Workflow:
- ✅ One active PR per agent targeting appropriate branch
- ✅ PR branches exist only while PR is open
- ✅ All work flows through PR
- ✅ Sequential PR merging (one at a time)
- ✅ Automatic cleanup keeps only `main` and `beta`

### Pre-commit Hooks:
- ✅ CMake flag validator
- ✅ AI-assisted review (optional)
- ✅ Hooks run automatically
- ✅ **AUTOMATIC** auto-fix and re-run on failure
- ✅ **NO USER INTERVENTION**

### CI/CD Integration:
- ✅ GitHub Actions workflow
- ✅ Automated validation checks on PR/push
- ✅ Comprehensive coverage

---

## AUTOMATION BEST PRACTICES

### Maximum Automation:
- ✅ Auto-fix all errors
- ✅ Auto-resolve conflicts
- ✅ Auto-merge when clean
- ✅ Auto-cleanup
- ✅ Auto-classify commits

### User Intervention ONLY When:
- Multiple solution options exist with different pros/cons
- **RARE** auto-resolution failure with multiple valid strategies

### AUTOMATIC HANDLING:
- All validation errors → Auto-fixed
- All critical issues → Auto-resolved
- All merges → Performed automatically
- All fixes → Batched → Single commit → Single push
- **BATCHING CRITICAL** for clean build tree

---

## USER COMMUNICATION BEST PRACTICES

### Feedback Level:
- ✅ Immediate status updates
- ✅ Adequate detail (not overwhelming)
- ✅ Clear error messages
- ✅ Summary format "Action → Result → Next Step"
- ✅ Avoid atomic-level details
- ✅ Always include repository name and path

### Communication Principles:
- ✅ Informational only (not asking permission)
- ✅ Report what was done, not what will be done (unless truly needed)
- ✅ Use consistent format for all updates
- ✅ Provide context for errors
- ✅ Show automation in action

---

## BUILD TREE CLEANLINESS

### Branch Management:
- ✅ Only `main` and `beta` remain after cleanup
- ✅ PR branches deleted immediately
- ✅ PRs closed immediately
- ✅ No orphaned branches

### File Management:
- ✅ Only core files modified
- ✅ No temporary files
- ✅ No patch scripts
- ✅ No workflow files

### Commit/Push Management:
- ✅ **BATCH ALL FIXES** → **SINGLE COMMIT** → **SINGLE PUSH**
- ✅ Clean history
- ✅ Minimal pushes
- ✅ One push per validation cycle

---

## EARLY ERROR DETECTION

### Multi-Layer Validation:
- ✅ Pre-commit hooks (early detection)
- ✅ Post-push agent validation (thorough check)
- ✅ CI/CD checks (continuous monitoring)

### Auto-fix Capabilities:
- ✅ Comprehensive checklists
- ✅ Multiple validation stages
- ✅ Clear error messages
- ✅ Automatic retry logic

---

## CONTEXT MANAGEMENT

### Rule File Size:
- ✅ Keep every rule file under 500 lines (preferably under 400)
- ✅ Chunk large instructions into targeted references
- ✅ Summarize long outputs before re-posting
- ✅ Trim optional flags/comments for concise content

### Context Optimization:
- ✅ Monitor conversation length
- ✅ Move artifacts to rule files (within size limits)
- ✅ Use cross-references instead of duplication
- ✅ Focus each module on specific topic

---

## ERROR RECOVERY PROCEDURES

### Auto-fix Retry Logic:
- ✅ Maximum 3 attempts per validation cycle
- ✅ Automatic retry on transient errors
- ✅ Escalation after 3 failed attempts

### Timeout Handling:
- ✅ Pre-commit hooks: 60 second timeout
- ✅ Post-push validation: 300 second timeout
- ✅ CoT audit: 600 second timeout
- ✅ Report timeout, suggest manual review if needed

### API Failure Handling:
- ✅ Fall back to basic validation if AI review fails
- ✅ Fall back to secondary provider if primary fails
- ✅ Report error, suggest manual review if all providers fail

